# =============================================================================
# fac omnia — marks a handled attempt for deferred kernel-error delivery.
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • `omnia` between `fac` and the block marks a handled block to defer
#     recoverable dispatched-kernel errors to its owning `cape` handler
#   • delivery can be observed before block exit when a host read synchronizes
#     device data; fatal device faults are not made catchable by this marker
#   • the modifier round-trips the formatter — re-emission keeps `fac omnia`
#   • this host-only example's synchronous `iace` still resolves through `cape`
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • expecting the marker to alter ordinary host control flow or catch fatal
#     device faults — it changes runner/device delivery of recoverable kernel
#     errors in a handled block, not host `iace`/`throw` behavior
#   • assuming `fac omnia { … } dum cond` is rejected — it is legal; deferred
#     errors are delivered per iteration when an owning handled block exists
#     (see fac-omnia-dum)
#
# See also: fac, cape, omnia
# =============================================================================

# fac omnia — caught iace resolves through the cape handler
#
# GRAMMAR:
#   facStmt :← 'fac' 'omnia'? block cape_clause?
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   1 / 0 / planum
#
# BACKEND:
#   Runner/device: dispatched failable kernels defer recoverable errors to the
#   owning handled block; fatal faults are not caught. Rust and host-only paths
#   do not use this device-delivery mechanism. This example's `iace` is a
#   synchronous host error and demonstrates ordinary `cape` handling only.

functio conatus(bivalens malum) → numerus {
    fac omnia {
        si malum {
            iace "casu"
        }
        redde 1
    }
    cape err {
        redde 0
    }
}

incipit {
    nota conatus(falsum)
    nota conatus(verum)
    fac omnia {
        nota "planum"
    }
    cape err {
        nota "interceptum"
    }
}
